# Facet — Game state

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the game's state entirely.

The one contract fixed over the state is the debugging and automation surface
specified for this build. Every operation that surface carries is a read or a
pose of the running game, so the state holds what those operations read, honors
what they write, and returns to its title-screen values when the surface's
`reset` is called. Beyond satisfying that surface, how the state is organized is
yours to decide.

Whatever its shape, the state carries the following:

- The screen the game is showing, and the item highlighted on whichever vertical
  menu that screen shows, counted from `0`. The snapshot reports it as
  `menuIndex`.
- The board being played: its `cols` and `rows`, and every cell of it with the
  kind, the cut, and the strain of the gem it holds. A cell's center on the
  stage comes from the cell center formulas in `specs/board.md` rather than from
  the state.
- The round's figures: the score, the level counted from `1`, and the score
  banked toward the current level. The level's target is `LEVEL_TARGET_STEP`
  (`2000`) times the level rather than a stored figure.
- Where resolution stands: the phase, `idle` or `resolving`; the chain step,
  which is `0` while the phase is `idle`; and the game time accumulated toward
  the next step. The chain multiplier is `min(chainStep, MAX_MULTIPLIER)`,
  `MAX_MULTIPLIER` (`8`), rather than a stored figure.
- What the most recent chain step did: how many cells it cleared and how many
  points it scored.
- The cell the keyboard cursor is on, the selected cell when one is selected,
  and the refused pair when a swap has just been refused, together with the game
  time that refusal has stood for.
- The pointer position, in the stage's logical units, and whether the pointer is
  currently pressed, both refreshed from the runtime layer in every update.
- The simulation time, in seconds, which every update adds its `dt` to whatever
  the screen, the menus included. The snapshot reports it as `simTime`.
- The runtime's mute bit, as the game's readable copy of it.
- The state of the seeded random generator, which the deal of an opening board
  and the refill in R9 draw from.

Whether a legal swap exists on the board is derived from R1 and R3 in
`specs/rules.md` rather than stored, so it answers to the board as it stands.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The state is a value the engine holds and every frame replaces, so every
field and every array is declared `readonly`: a frame builds the next state from
the current one rather than writing into it, and every reader is handed the
state as `DeepReadonly<FacetState>` in any case.

```ts
export type GemKind =
  | "ruby"
  | "amber"
  | "citrine"
  | "jade"
  | "beryl"
  | "sapphire"
  | "amethyst";

export type Cut = "plain" | "brilliant" | "star" | "prism";

export type Screen = "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "paused" | "gameover";

export type Phase = "idle" | "resolving";

export interface CellRef {
  readonly col: number;
  readonly row: number;
}

export interface GemState {
  readonly col: number;
  readonly row: number;
  readonly kind: GemKind | null;
  readonly cut: Cut;
  readonly strain: number;
}

export interface BoardState {
  readonly cols: number;
  readonly rows: number;
  readonly cells: readonly GemState[];
}

export interface RefusalState {
  readonly a: CellRef;
  readonly b: CellRef;
  readonly timer: number;
}

export interface PointerState {
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly down: boolean;
}

export interface FacetState {
  readonly screen: Screen;
  readonly menuIndex: number;

  readonly board: BoardState;
  readonly phase: Phase;
  readonly chainStep: number;
  readonly stepTimer: number;

  readonly score: number;
  readonly level: number;
  readonly levelScore: number;
  readonly lastCleared: number;
  readonly lastPoints: number;

  readonly cursor: CellRef;
  readonly selection: CellRef | null;
  readonly refusal: RefusalState | null;

  readonly pointer: PointerState;
  readonly simTime: number;
  readonly muted: boolean;
  readonly rngState: number;
}
```

## The type aliases

`GemKind` names one gem identity, and the seven are the members of `GEM_KINDS`
in that order, as `specs/board.md` defines them. `Cut` names the four cuts a gem
can carry, `plain` being the cut a dealt gem arrives with and the other three
being the cuts a run creates under R8 in `specs/rules.md`. `Screen` is the
top-level state machine, and `screen` names the screen the game is showing;
every build carries all five. `Phase` names where resolution stands on the
`playing` screen: `idle` while the board is settled and `resolving` while a
chain is running. `CellRef` addresses one cell of the board, `col` and `row`
both zero-indexed from the top-left.

## `GemState`

One gem and the cell it occupies. The board carries one entry per cell, in
reading order from the top-left cell to the bottom-right.

- `col`, `row` — the cell the gem occupies.
- `kind` — the gem's kind, and `null` for a `prism`, which carries no kind.
- `cut` — `"plain"`, `"brilliant"`, `"star"`, or `"prism"`.
- `strain` — the gem's strain, a whole number from `0` to `MAX_STRAIN` (`3`). A
  gem at `MAX_STRAIN` is flawed.

A gem's center on the stage is `cellX(col)` and `cellY(row)` from
`specs/board.md`, so the state carries no coordinates. Strain and cut travel
with the gem when R9 drops it into a lower cell, because they are the gem's
rather than the cell's.

## `BoardState`

The board being played, and `{ cols: 0, rows: 0, cells: [] }` while no board is
in play.

- `cols`, `rows` — the grid's dimensions, `GRID_COLS` (`8`) by `GRID_ROWS` (`8`)
  whenever a board is in play.
- `cells` — every cell of the board, in reading order from the top-left cell to
  the bottom-right, so `cells` holds `cols * rows` entries. Every cell holds a
  gem once a chain step has finished settling under R9.

## `RefusalState`

The swap most recently refused under R1, R2, or R3, and `null` whenever none is
standing.

- `a`, `b` — the two cells the refused swap named, in the order they were named.
- `timer` — the game time the refusal has stood for, in seconds. Every update
  adds its `dt` to it, and `refusal` returns to `null` once it reaches
  `REFUSAL_SECONDS` (`0.3`).

## `PointerState`

Where the pointer is and whether it is pressed, as the game read it in the
current frame.

- `x`, `y` — the pointer position in the stage's logical units, the same units
  the cell centers are in, so a hit test against a cell center needs no
  conversion.
- `down` — whether the pointer is currently pressed. A press farther than
  `GEM_HIT_R` (`36`) from every cell center leaves `down` `true` and both
  `selection` and `board` as they stand.

## `FacetState`

The whole of Facet's state. Every field is present from the moment `initialize`
returns, and every one is plain data: numbers, strings, booleans, and containers
of them, so a board is posed by building a state that differs in the fields
posed and read back by reading them.

- `screen` — the screen currently shown.
- `menuIndex` — the highlighted item on whichever vertical menu `screen` is
  showing.
- `board` — the board being played.
- `phase` — `"idle"` while the board is settled, `"resolving"` while a chain is
  running.
- `chainStep` — the chain step being resolved, counted from `1`, and `0` while
  `phase` is `"idle"`.
- `stepTimer` — the game time accumulated toward the next chain step, in
  seconds, which returns to `0` at `STEP_SECONDS` (`0.25`) as `specs/rules.md`
  describes.
- `score` — the round's score.
- `level` — the level being played, counted from `1`.
- `levelScore` — the score banked toward the current level, which returns to `0`
  when the level advances.
- `lastCleared` — how many cells the most recent chain step cleared. Each step
  replaces it, and it holds `0` until a step has run.
- `lastPoints` — how many points the most recent chain step scored. Each step
  replaces it, and it holds `0` until a step has run.
- `cursor` — the cell the keyboard cursor is on, both coordinates within the
  board's dimensions.
- `selection` — the selected cell, or `null` when nothing is selected.
- `refusal` — the refusal currently standing, or `null`.
- `pointer` — the pointer as of this frame.
- `simTime` — accumulated simulation time, in seconds. Every `update` adds its
  `dt`, whatever the screen, the menus included.
- `muted` — the game's readable copy of the engine's mute bit, which every
  `update` carries into the state it returns. The engine owns muting, and this
  field is what the debug surface reports.
- `rngState` — the state of the game's seeded random generator. A `reset` with a
  seed sets it, so reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same
  result. The deal of an opening board and the refill in R9 draw from it.

Four facts the game shows are derived rather than stored. A cell's center comes
from the cell center formulas in `specs/board.md`. The level's target is
`LEVEL_TARGET_STEP` (`2000`) times `level`. The chain multiplier is
`min(chainStep, MAX_MULTIPLIER)`, `MAX_MULTIPLIER` (`8`). Whether a legal swap
exists comes from R1 and R3 in `specs/rules.md`, read over the board as it
stands.

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
- `initialize` builds the whole state in one go, so every field is present by
  the time a frame can observe it, and none of them is optional.
- Fields you add hold derived data you can rebuild from the declared ones. A
  `reset` restores the declared fields to their title-screen values, so those
  fields are the whole of the authoritative state.
- The state handed to `update` is the whole of the game, and the state `update`
  returns is the whole of the next frame. Every value the game carries from one
  frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a module-level variable or a
  closure.
- The state is handed over read-only, as `DeepReadonly<FacetState>`, to
  `update`, to `render`, to every diagnostic source, and to every operation of
  the debug surface. `update` and the surface's poses return a new `FacetState`
  built from the one they received; `render`, the sources, and `snapshot` return
  no state.
{{else}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against. This
section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it stands
here. The state is the world's game state: a class extending the engine's
`GameState`, named by the game mode as its `gameStateClass`, so the engine
builds one instance of it when the world opens and `engine.world.state` is that
instance. Wherever this specification writes `state.foo`, it means a field of
that instance.

The game runs in one world for the whole session. The game definition registers
a single level, the game never opens another, and every screen is a value of
`screen`, so the state below is built once and lives until the page closes. The
framework's states are live objects: a tick writes the fields it advances in
place, and each field initializer below is that field's title-screen value, the
same value the debug surface's `reset` restores. `GameState` comes from the
engine's package, and `DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`) is the seed a `reset` applies when it
is given none.

```ts
export type GemKind =
  | "ruby"
  | "amber"
  | "citrine"
  | "jade"
  | "beryl"
  | "sapphire"
  | "amethyst";

export type Cut = "plain" | "brilliant" | "star" | "prism";

export type Screen = "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "paused" | "gameover";

export type Phase = "idle" | "resolving";

export interface CellRef {
  col: number;
  row: number;
}

export interface GemState {
  col: number;
  row: number;
  kind: GemKind | null;
  cut: Cut;
  strain: number;
}

export interface BoardState {
  cols: number;
  rows: number;
  cells: GemState[];
}

export interface RefusalState {
  a: CellRef;
  b: CellRef;
  timer: number;
}

export interface PointerState {
  x: number;
  y: number;
  down: boolean;
}

export class FacetState extends GameState {
  screen: Screen = "title";
  menuIndex = 0;

  board: BoardState = { cols: 0, rows: 0, cells: [] };
  phase: Phase = "idle";
  chainStep = 0;
  stepTimer = 0;

  score = 0;
  level = 1;
  levelScore = 0;
  lastCleared = 0;
  lastPoints = 0;

  cursor: CellRef = { col: 0, row: 0 };
  selection: CellRef | null = null;
  refusal: RefusalState | null = null;

  pointer: PointerState = { x: 0, y: 0, down: false };
  simTime = 0;
  muted = false;
  rngState = DEFAULT_SEED;
}
```

