`FacetState` extends the engine's `GameState`, so an instance also carries the
framework's own `world`, `players`, `phase`, and `elapsed`. Facet declares its
own `phase` over the framework's: the field above is the resolution phase, the
mode never calls `setPhase`, and `elapsed` stays `0`. The one player the mode
adds is `players[0]`, whose controller is where input is read.

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
- The engine builds the state when the world opens, through the mode's
  `gameStateClass`, so every field is present by the time a frame can observe
  it, and none of them is optional.
- The state is the whole of the authoritative game. Every value the game carries
  from one frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a module-level
  variable or a closure, and any field an actor, component, or controller holds
  of its own is derived data rebuilt from the declared ones.
- The state is written only through the paths play runs on: the player
  controller resolves the frame's input into it, the game mode's tick advances
  it, and the debug surface's poses arrange it at the call. Rendering reads it
  and writes nothing, and whether a swap is accepted and what a chain step
  clears are decided from `board` alone.
- A `reset` restores the declared fields to the initializers above, so those
  fields are the whole of the authoritative state.
{{/if}}
